							 22-Dec-1997

This is a SIPP patch for DIR.SAV in RT-11 V5.1C ONLY.  To use it, verify
that you have an unpatched, unmodified copy of DIR.SAV (with a July 1984
file date), preferably from your distribution diskettes.  You may also
wish to verify the size as noted in block 0, location 50; it should be 200
less than what the SIPP file will change it to or 22362 (octal).  Then,
just type:

COPY DIR.SAV DK:DIR.51C
UNPROT DIR.51C
@DIR51C.SIP
REN DIR.SAV DIR.OLD
REN DIR.51C DIR.SAV

The patch extends the function of the /VOLUME switch to DIR
(which can still be abbreviated as desired).

The syntax is:

DIR/VOLUME:ONLY       as before or
DIR/VOLUME:<Starting Block Number>[:<Nested Starting Block Number>]
which makes it very easy when SL: is set on - no need to add numbers.

The one additional trick is when the <Starting Block Number> is:
24572. = 57774 (octal) = ^rONL (RAD50)
In the unlikely event that does happen, just enter:
DIR/VOLUME:24000.:572.
or any other such combination which adds up to 24572.

The reason for this is that the ONLY and block number switches cannot be
combined at the same time.

Note that the second and subsequent block numbers are _nested_ block
numbers, so you do not need to add offsets on the original device in your
head.  For example, if there is a logical disk on DL0:, issue a DIR/BLOCK
command to find its starting block number (SBN).  If the SBN is 10000., then:

DIR/VOLUME:10000. DL0:

will read that logical disk.  If you copied or created another logical disk
on that logical disk, and DIR/BLOCK/OCTAL shows it's on block 300, then

DIR/VOLUME:10000.:300

will list that logical disk.  You can nest as many disks as you want, even
if your LD: driver can only handle 8 logical disks (LD drivers prior to V5.5
are limited to 8 logical disks, LD0: through LD7:).  Note that the above
command mixes octal SBN's (no period) and decimal SBN's
(with a period).  A hypothetical example of extreme but legal nesting
would be:

DIR/VOLUME:10000:300:24:21:75:89.:10.:22:22

which would list a ninth-level logical disk that could not be accessed on
RT-11 versions prior to V5.5.

Uses
====

1. Quickly listing directories without needing to mount the logical disk.
2. Having more than eight logical disks in use at one time.
3. Listing the directory of a deleted logical disk, perhaps to determine
   whether to undelete it with COPY/DEVICE ddn:/START:a/END:b foo.dsk/FILE
4. Anything else you find useful.

